      <h1 class="post-header-title">How I got <em>here</em>.</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>I'm a Portuguese strategist and writer. The thing I'm trying to do, with everything I write and ship, is translate complex systems into narratives that shape how decisions get made.</p>

      <h2>The day job</h2>

      <p>I work on operational transformation at <strong>Fidelidade</strong>, Portugal's largest insurer. Issue trees, process mining, executive presentations, the work of helping a 220-year-old institution absorb digital change. It's where the writing earns its credibility on institutions and operations. You learn things inside an institution you cannot learn writing about one from outside.</p>

      <h2>The night work</h2>

      <p>By night I write essays and build the agentic systems I write about. Two of those: <strong>bernie-brand-os</strong> (a personal-brand content system that runs as a continuous loop) and <strong>Noxx</strong> (a multi-author content automation for three founders). Both are in production, both are documented in public, both inform the writing. I'm a co-founder at <strong>IndieBacked</strong>, where we back builders working at the intersection of insurance, AI, and infrastructure.</p>

      <h2>The arc</h2>

      <p>The longer arc is institutional. There's a four-phase path I keep in mind: <em>foundation</em> (writing, thinking, public body of work), <em>authority</em> (frameworks, essays, recognised analysis), <em>advisory</em> (consulting, decision-making rooms), <em>institutional influence</em> (policy, governance, public work). I'm in phase one. The route there goes through writing first, advising second, institutional work third. There are no shortcuts.</p>

      <h2>What I write about</h2>

      <p>Five overlapping pillars: <em>technology and institutions</em> (how AI reshapes how organisations and economies actually work), <em>productivity and the agent-economy</em> (what changes when agents become labour), <em>building in public</em> (the systems I ship and what breaks), <em>insurance and infrastructure</em> (the industry I work in by day), and <em>Portugal</em> (the country I want to see catch up).</p>

      <h2>Where I came from</h2>

      <p>Born in Porto in 1992. Six years in Tokyo before Lisbon. Native Portuguese and French, fluent English, conversational Spanish. Trained as a strategist; learned to ship as a builder.</p>

      <h2>How to reach me</h2>

      <p>Email is the highest signal: <a href="mailto:bernardo@bernardofaro.com">bernardo@bernardofaro.com</a>. I read everything that doesn't pitch me. The newsletter is <a href="/#newsletter">Mental Models for the Agent‑Economy</a>. The shorter notes are on <a href="https://x.com/hey_bernie">X</a> and <a href="https://linkedin.com/in/bernardofaro">LinkedIn</a>.</p>
